WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.860
Before we start, I’d like to tell you that
this video is not about a paper, and it is

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.680
not going to be two minutes.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.500
Welcome to Two Minute Papers!

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.780
This piece bears the name “The Bitter Lesson”
and was written by Richard Sutton, a legendary

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:23.020
Canadian researcher who has contributed a
great deal to reinforcement learning research.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.760
And what a piece this is!

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.840
It is a short article on how we should do
research, and ever since I read it, I couldn’t

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.970
stop thinking about it, and as a result, I
couldn’t not make a video on this topic.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.190
We really have to talk about this.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:42.640
It takes less than 5 minutes to read, so before
we talk about it, you can pause the video

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.210
and click the link to it in the video description.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.820
So, in this article, he makes two important
observations.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.719
Number one, he argues that the best performing
learning techniques are the ones that can

00:00:54.719 --> 00:01:01.040
leverage computation, or, in other words,
methods that improve significantly as we add

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.309
more compute power.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:08.571
Long ago, people tried to encode lots of human
knowledge of strategies in their Go AI, but

00:01:08.571 --> 00:01:13.119
did not have enough compute power to make
a truly great algorithm.

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:19.079
And now we have AlphaGo, which contains minimal
information about Go itself, and it is better

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.720
than the best human players in the world.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.700
And, number two, he recommends that we try
to put as few constraints on a problem as

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.020
possible.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:34.189
He argues that we shouldn't try to rebuild
the mind, but try to build a method that can

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:38.539
capture arbitrary complexity and scale it
up with hardware.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:44.039
Don’t try to make it work like your brain
- make something as general as possible and

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:49.169
make sure it can leverage computation and
it will come up with something that is way

00:01:49.169 --> 00:01:50.670
better than our brain.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:56.149
So, in short, keep the problem general, and
don’t encode your knowledge of the domain

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.439
into your learning algorithms.

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:04.270
The weight of this sentence is not to be underestimated,
because these seemingly simple observations

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.380
sound really counterintuitive.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:12.650
This seemingly encourages us to do the exact
opposite of what we are currently doing.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:13.950
Let me tell you why.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.830
I have fond memories of my early lectures
I attended to in cryptography, where we had

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.140
a look at ciphertexts.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.400
These are very much like encrypted messages
that children like to write each other at

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.620
school, which looks like nonsense for the
unassuming teacher, but can be easily decoded

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.050
by another child when provided with a key.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:39.130
This key describes which symbol corresponds
to which letter.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:45.020
Let’s assume that one symbol means one letter,
but if we don’t have any additional knowledge,

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.120
this is still not an easy problem to crack.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.230
But in this course, soon, we coded up algorithms
that were able to crack messages like this

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.620
in less than a second.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.740
How exactly?

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:01.050
Well, by inserting additional knowledge into
the system.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.740
For instance, we know the relative frequency
of each letter in every language.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:11.400
For instance, in English, the letter “e”
is the most common by far, and then comes

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.210
“t”, “a”, and the others.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:19.370
The fact that we are not seeing letters but
symbols doesn’t really matter, because we

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:25.300
just look up the most frequent symbol in the
ciphertext and we immediately know that okay,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.120
that symbol is going to be the letter “e”,
and so on.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.900
See what we have done here?

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.330
Just by inserting a tiny bit of knowledge,
suddenly, a very difficult problem turned

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.450
into a trivial problem.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:43.560
So much so that anyone can implement this
after their second cryptography lecture.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.080
And somehow, Richard Sutton argues that we
shouldn’t do that?

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.240
Doesn’t that sound crazy?

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.330
So, what gives?

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:56.040
Well, let me explain through an example from
light transport research that demonstrates

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.450
his point.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:03.260
Path tracing is one of the first and simplest
algorithms in the field, which, in many regards,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.440
is vastly inferior to Metropolis Light Transport,
which is a much smarter algorithm.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:14.080
However, with our current powerful graphics
cards, we can compute so many more rays with

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.539
path tracing, that in many cases it wins over
Metropolis.

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:20.879
In this case, compute reigns supreme.

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:26.419
The hardware scaling outmuscles the smarts,
and we haven’t even talked about how much

00:04:26.419 --> 00:04:32.020
easier it is for engineers to maintain and
improve a simpler system.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.710
The area of Natural Language Processing has
many decades of research to teach machines

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:43.419
how to understand, simplify, correct, or even
generate text.

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:48.469
After so many papers and handcrafted techniques
which insert our knowledge of linguistics

00:04:48.469 --> 00:04:53.710
into our techniques, who would have thought
that OpenAI would be able to come up with

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:59.039
a relatively simple neural network with so
little prior knowledge that is able to write

00:04:59.039 --> 00:05:01.870
articles that sound remarkably lifelike.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:06.520
We will talk about this method in more detail
in this series soon.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.030
And here comes the bitter lesson.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:14.740
Doing research the classical way of inserting
knowledge into a solution is very satisfying

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:20.699
- it feels right, it feels like doing research,
progressing, and it makes it easy to show

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:24.990
in a new paper what exactly the key contributions
are.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.699
However, it may not be the most effective
way forward.

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:29.969
Quoting the article.

00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:33.059
I recommend that you pay close attention to
this.

00:05:33.059 --> 00:05:39.699
“The bitter lesson is based on the historical
observations that 1) AI researchers have often

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:45.569
tried to build knowledge into their agents,
2) this always helps in the short term, and

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:52.259
is personally satisfying to the researcher,
but 3) in the long run it plateaus and even

00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:58.759
inhibits further progress, and 4) breakthrough
progress eventually arrives by an opposing

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:03.580
approach based on scaling computation by search
and learning.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:09.380
The eventual success is tinged with bitterness,
and often incompletely digested, because its

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.050
success over a favored, human-centric approach.“

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.870
In our cryptography problem from earlier,
of course the letter frequency solution and

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.169
other linguistic tricks are clearly much,
much better than a solution that doesn’t

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:25.219
know anything about the domain.

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:26.219
Of course!

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:33.129
However, when later, we have a 100 times faster
hardware, this knowledge may actually inhibit

00:06:33.129 --> 00:06:36.159
finding a solution that is way, way better.

00:06:36.159 --> 00:06:40.760
This is why he also claims that we shouldn’t
try to build intelligence by modeling our

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.699
brain in a computer simulation.

00:06:42.699 --> 00:06:47.449
It’s not that the “our brain” approach
doesn’t work - it does, but on the short

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:48.449
run.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:53.460
On the long run, we will be able to add more
hardware to a learning algorithm, and it will

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:59.900
find more effective structures to solve problems,
and it will eventually outmuscle our handcrafted

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.110
techniques.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:06.229
In short, this is the lesson: when facing
a learning problem, keep your domain knowledge

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:09.520
out of the solution, and use more compute.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.669
More compute gives us more learning, and more
general formulations give us more chance to

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:16.539
find something relevant.

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:19.120
So, this is indeed a harsh lesson.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.669
This piece sparked great debates on Twitter,
I have seen great points for and against this

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:25.879
sentiment.

00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:26.979
What do you think?

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:31.009
Let me know in the comments, as everything
in science, this piece should be subject to

00:07:31.009 --> 00:07:32.879
debate and criticism.

00:07:32.879 --> 00:07:36.770
And therefore, I’d love to read as many
people’s take on it as possible.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:42.180
And, this piece has implications on my thinking
as well — please allow me to add a three

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.630
more small personal notes that kept me up
at night in the last few days.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:51.580
Note number one, the bottom line is whenever
we build a new algorithm, we should always

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:57.759
bear in mind which parts would be truly useful
if we had a 100 times the compute power that

00:07:57.759 --> 00:07:59.430
we have now.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:04.889
Note number two, a corollary of this thinking
is that arguably, hardware engineers who make

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:10.419
these new and more powerful graphics cards
may be contributing the very least as much

00:08:10.419 --> 00:08:13.919
to AI than most of AI research does.

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:19.469
And note number three, to me it feels like
this almost implies that best is to join the

00:08:19.469 --> 00:08:22.330
big guys where all the best hardware is.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:27.789
I work in an amazing, small to mid-sized lab
at the Technical University of Vienna and

00:08:27.789 --> 00:08:33.020
in the last few years, I have given relatively
little consideration to the invitations from

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:36.510
some of the more coveted and well-funded labs.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.910
Was it a mistake?

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:38.910
Should I change that?

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.390
I really don’t know for sure.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:45.100
If for some reason, you haven’t read the
piece at the start of the video, make sure

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.780
to do it after watching this.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.280
It’s really worth it.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:54.190
In the meantime, interestingly, the non-profit
AI research lab, OpenAI also established a

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:59.600
for profit, or what they like to call “capped
profit” company to be able to compete with

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.320
the other big guys like DeepMind and Facebook
Reality Labs.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:06.110
I think Richard has a solid point here.

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.420
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

